#: src/app/gesicht_app.py
msgid ""
"Your face data (numbers, no photos) is then stored on the disk only "
"encrypted. The key is in this computer’s TPM chip. When the computer starts, "
"Winky unseals the data into memory only, so unlocking stays just as fast.\n"
"\n"
"This helps if someone reads your disk in another computer or from Windows on "
"the same computer (dual boot). It doesn’t help if someone starts this "
"computer from a USB stick with Ubuntu or a related Linux: Secure Boot "
"accepts it, so the TPM chip unseals the data there too. A BIOS password that "
"blocks starting from USB helps against that. If your disk is already "
"encrypted, sealing adds little.\n"
"\n"
"Please note: after a BIOS or firmware update, or if Secure Boot is turned on "
"or off, the TPM chip may refuse the data. Winky then tells you, and you "
"record your face again. Your password always works. The previous unencrypted "
"data is deleted; on SSDs, remnants can’t be ruled out completely, so record "
"your face again afterwards if that matters to you."
msgstr ""

